#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325534 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f5629e6-d958-5a8e-8c2b-b77a15f25f1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325536} {:answer/id #uuid "72ef936d-4929-5b14-b256-76ff1b5efb6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325537} {:answer/id #uuid "4ba7bac5-9c09-59dc-a0b1-b8274e5e8825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325538} {:answer/id #uuid "869f9e34-00e3-5e66-8361-3a5db185d1d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325539} {:answer/id #uuid "4dbaa03e-88bf-5d85-9cda-12ee90f9bfb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325540}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f5629e6-d958-5a8e-8c2b-b77a15f25f1f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325536} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f539163-fa77-5396-898b-2e48d80e3e10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325535}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2dd6f11c-4839-54cb-8c9d-7c7da33b80c8" :question/math-academy-id "q-335247" :question/problem "For which of the following functions is it valid to differentiate under the integral sign?\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{x+t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{x-t}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>1$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each function:\n\n- For the first function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x,t)=\\sqrt{x+t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{1}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{1}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x+t}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R$ (since $x+t\\ge c>0$ on $R$). Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{1}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the second function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{2}(x,t)=\\frac{xt}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>0$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(0,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that ${f}_{2}$ is continuous on $R$ (since the denominator never vanishes on $R$). Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{2}}{∂t}=\\frac{x({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})-2x{t}^{2}}{({x}^{2}+{t}^{2})^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{2}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>0.$\n\n- For the third function, we have \n\n$$\n{f}_{3}(x,t)=\\frac{1}{x-t}.\n$$\n\nWe fix ${t}_{0}>1$ and choose $[c,d]⊂(1,\\infty )$ such that $c<{t}_{0}<d,$ so $R=[0,1]\\times [c,d].$\n\nNote that for $t>1$ and $x\\in [0,1],$ the denominator $x-t$ never vanishes on $R,$ so ${f}_{3}$ is continuous on $R.$ Computing the partial derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂{f}_{3}}{∂t}=\\frac{1}{(x-t)^{2}},\n$$\n\nwhich exists and is continuous on $R.$ Thus, differentiating under the integral sign is valid at ${t}_{0}.$ And since our choice of ${t}_{0}$ was arbitrary, we can compute ${F}_{3}^{′}(t)$ for all real $t>1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374079 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bbefb3bb-4783-5d77-b404-1f43ee8765de" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Cases Where Differentiating Under the Integral Sign Applies"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d65fc7-222b-57df-af18-d5f179328795" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374081} {:answer/id #uuid "00c9eb58-0f77-54ed-b99a-75d7ea415170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374082} {:answer/id #uuid "72547652-05c5-564c-9e68-1ce3e6081bc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374083} {:answer/id #uuid "52bff97b-d237-5101-b351-f70e38d15ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "None" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374084} {:answer/id #uuid "c2033edc-46d0-5266-ab71-615b97de26c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374085}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "00c9eb58-0f77-54ed-b99a-75d7ea415170" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374082} :answer-field/id #uuid "dca1d5cf-effe-5489-9ee3-4eb786f87d58" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374080}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9642dfed-e959-50ee-bb3a-bb0e4c6f718b" :question/math-academy-id "q-335252" :question/problem "For which of the following functions does the continuous-partial-derivative sufficient theorem apply throughout the stated parameter domain? Apply the theorem locally on a rectangle $[0,1]\\times[c,d]$: the integrand and its partial derivative with respect to $t$ must both be continuous there, and the partial derivative must exist at every point of the rectangle.\n\n- ${F}_{1}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{-{t}^{2}x}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\in R$\n- ${F}_{2}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}|x-t|\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t\\ge 0$\n- ${F}_{3}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x,\\,t>0$" :question/worked-solution "The stated sufficient theorem applies to I and III. For I, the partial derivative is $-2txe^{-t^2x}$; for III, it is $-2t/(x^2+t^2)^2$. Both integrands and their partial derivatives are continuous on a closed rectangle around every allowed parameter value, taking $c>0$ for III.\n\nFor II, $\\partial_t|x-t|$ equals $-1$ when $x>t$ and $1$ when $x<t$, but does not exist at $x=t$. Thus the stated theorem does not apply throughout its parameter domain. Its failure does not prove that interchange is invalid: the difference quotients are bounded in absolute value by $1$ and converge for almost every $x$, so dominated convergence justifies differentiation under the integral for II. Explicitly, $F_2(t)=t^2-t+1/2$ for $0\\le t\\le1$ and $F_2(t)=t-1/2$ for $t\\ge1$, giving $F_2′(t)=2t-1$ on $(0,1)$ and $1$ for $t\\ge1$, with right derivative $-1$ at $0$.\n\nUnder the explicit sufficient-theorem request, the answer is I and III only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379200 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65338c66-e928-50a3-9616-fbfda716827b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20t^4+10t^9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379202} {:answer/id #uuid "5bd545d1-de75-59e3-a4de-b5e07bb5d71b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20t^4+10t^5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "65338c66-e928-50a3-9616-fbfda716827b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20t^4+10t^9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379202} :answer-field/id #uuid "b01e1ddf-a21a-5597-a6fc-26a62b71e64a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379201}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "11d0068f-c50c-56c9-ac9d-c760f1b21cef" :question/math-academy-id "q-350548" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4x+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4x+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4x+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4x+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4x+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(4x+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}4x\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}{|}_{0}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =2+{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2+{t}^{5}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379204 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69a0b7ad-9c2f-5f95-bde4-a3524ee2abb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}t^3+16t^7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379206}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69a0b7ad-9c2f-5f95-bde4-a3524ee2abb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{64}{3}t^3+16t^7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379206} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0686e5e-b3bc-5db8-a7fd-a2659198ecb5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379205}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "47e13c43-3d7e-5887-8a06-229459d7d60b" :question/math-academy-id "q-350550" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})^{2}] & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\cdot 4{t}^{3} \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}8{t}^{3}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{t}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({x}^{2}+{t}^{4})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{2}{t}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{3}}{3}{|}_{0}^{2}+{t}^{4}x{|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3}+2{t}^{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=8{t}^{3}(\\frac{8}{3}+2{t}^{4}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186379207 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "e829ae4a-7612-5e40-8214-aa56027c38be" :knowledge-point/title "Differentiating Under the Integral Sign"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "826bad29-8cab-576e-8fe5-c67f43909d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20t^9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "826bad29-8cab-576e-8fe5-c67f43909d9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20t^9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379209} :answer-field/id #uuid "ce38428c-6c3f-5e4a-8f35-9675de041abc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186379208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c72f5578-6a77-5982-9416-5354dbedb9f1" :question/math-academy-id "q-350551" :question/problem "Given that $I(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x,$ calculate ${I}^{′}(t)$ for $t\\in R$ by differentiating under the integral sign.\n\n${I}^{′}(t)=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose $f(x,t)$ is continuous on $R=[a,b]\\times [c,d]$ and $\\frac{∂f}{∂t}$ exists and is continuous on $R.$ Then, the function\n\n$$\nF(t)={\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nis differentiable on $(c,d),$ and\n\n$$\n{F}^{′}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\frac{∂f}{∂t}(x,t)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThis is known as *differentiating under the integral sign.*\n\nWe wish to compute ${I}^{′}(t),$ where\n\n$$\nI(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating under the integral sign, we get\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}]\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nNext, let's compute the partial derivative with respect to $t$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂}{∂t}[(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})^{2}] & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot \\frac{∂}{∂t}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5}) \\\\  & =2(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\cdot 5{t}^{4} \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this back into our integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{I}^{′}(t) & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}10{t}^{4}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =10{t}^{4}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let's evaluate the remaining integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}(4{x}^{3}+{t}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-1}^{1}4{x}^{3}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{t}^{5}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={x}^{4}{|}_{-1}^{1}+{t}^{5}x{|}_{-1}^{1} \\\\  & =(1-1)+({t}^{5}-(-{t}^{5})) \\\\  & =2{t}^{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{I}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}(2{t}^{5})=20{t}^{9}.\n$$"}]}
